 "narrative": {
  "northStar": "The Night Shift Ledger",
  "overview": "YNT ERP is a calm, exact ledger that a whole business works from all day. A fixed navy sidebar frames the work like the cover of an account book; inside it, white sheets (cards) hold the numbers in tidy rows. The sky behind the sheets changes with the theme: a warm daylight wash in the top-right in light mode, an animated starfield in dark mode. That atmosphere is the only decoration the system allows, and it always sits behind the work, never on it.\n\nDensity is high and deliberate. Staff confirm orders, read RTS rates and check payroll at speed, on desktops and phones, so type is small but steady (13–14px body, 11.5px uppercase labels), numbers line up in tabular figures, and colour is spent on meaning: blue means \"you can act here\", green/amber/red mean an outcome. The look is kept, not reinvented: refinements stay inside this world, and a page is only redesigned when the owner asks for that page.",
  "keyCharacteristics": [
   "Navy sidebar frame, white sheets, one blue action colour.",
   "Status colours carry order outcomes (delivered, pending, returned), never decoration.",
   "Soft-lifted cards with gently curved 12px corners.",
   "DM Sans throughout; DM Mono only for clocks, codes and scan fields.",
   "Two full themes (light and dark) with a living sky behind each."
  ],
  "rules": [
   {
    "name": "The One Blue Rule",
    "body": "Action Blue marks what can be clicked or what is active, and nothing else. Headers, chart fills and highlights do not borrow it.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Outcome Rule",
    "body": "Green, amber and red always mean an outcome (delivered, waiting, returned/failed). Never use them for decoration or category colour.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Steady Figures Rule",
    "body": "Every number that can be compared (money, counts, rates) uses tabular figures and stays on one line.",
    "section": "typography"
   },
   {
    "name": "The 16px Field Rule",
    "body": "On phones and touch screens every input, select and textarea is 16px, so iOS never zooms the page.",
    "section": "typography"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Soft Lift Rule",
    "body": "Cards are lifted at rest with the resting shadow plus their 1px rule line; they never use a coloured or glowing shadow. (The incumbent CSS still draws most cards flat; bring them up to the resting shadow as pages are touched.)",
    "section": "elevation"
   }
  ],
  "dos": [
   "**Do** use Action Blue (#3b82f6) only for buttons, links, focus and the active nav item.",
   "**Do** keep every comparable number in tabular figures on one line.",
   "**Do** give cards the resting shadow plus a 1px #e2e8f0 rule line and 12px corners.",
   "**Do** design both themes for every new surface; use the tokens, never hard-coded light colours.",
   "**Do** make phone layouts real: 16px fields, 44px touch targets, tables as cards under 640px.",
   "**Do** keep the sky (daylight wash, starfield) behind the work only."
  ],
  "donts": [
   "**Don't** use green, amber or red for anything but outcomes, and don't use the --success/--danger tokens as chart series colours (use #059669 / #ef4444).",
   "**Don't** add new decoration (gradient blobs, glows, gradient text, illustrated icon tiles) on top of the sheets.",
   "**Don't** introduce a second typeface; DM Mono stays limited to clocks, codes and scan fields.",
   "**Don't** replace the navy sidebar, the two themes or the starfield during a refinement."
  ]
 }
}